                    coach_default:          `Decide. The clock is the deadline, and a maybe past day 100 is itself a strategic loss.`,
                    coach_r1_digital:       `Good — you've named it as a digital-relevance problem. That diagnosis is what lets you defend a €130M yes later; cite e-commerce penetration alone and the prudent directors will eat you alive.`,
                    coach_r1_value:         `Value defence stops the discounters on price, but it leaves the firm irrelevant to how customers now shop. Is the real question price, or relevance?`,
                    coach_r1_scale:         `Protecting supplier terms and changing nothing else is exactly the culture of prudence that let rivals take 1.1 points last year. Scale follows relevance, not the other way round.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `“Keep optionality open and decide later” is the polite maybe the board hired you to break. Indecision past day 100 is itself a strategic loss.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `A sharp diagnosis: a digital-relevance problem, solved by a focused pair that compounds. Now you have a question your €220M can actually answer — and a yes you can defend.`,
                    coach_r1_copy:          `Copying both larger rivals feature for feature means matching, never winning — and you'll always be a year behind on someone else's roadmap. Where can €220M build advantage they can't copy back?`,
                    coach_r1_cheapest:      `Spending wherever it's cheapest preserves the EBIT margin and cedes the market quietly. The prudent-looking choice is the high-risk one here.`,
                    coach_r1_defer:         `“The data isn't clear enough” will be true on day 100, day 200 and the day you slip below #4. Leadership is committing before the data is complete.`,
                    coach_r1_hedge:         `A half-hearted commitment reads to the board as a maybe in a suit. The Chair wants a yes, not a hedge.`,

                    coach_r2_focused:       `Now that's a bet: a focused pair — the omnichannel rebuild for relevance, the data/loyalty engine for margin — that compounds. Rivals spend €200M on one thing; you've concentrated enough to win on two that reinforce each other.`,
                    coach_r2_staged:        `A focused pair, committed as a staged real option with a pre-committed scale trigger. That is the power of yes operationalised — decisive and flexible, not all-in and blind.`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `You funded a bit of everything — €55M each builds nothing that wins, while rivals spend €200M on one thing and beat you on it. Four polite maybes is not a strategy. Which TWO bets actually change Atlântico's position?`,
                    coach_r2_three:         `Three bets is closer, but the envelope barely funds two at winning scale — the third is starving the pair that matters. What are you brave enough to NOT do?`,
                    coach_r2_norelevance:   `Two bets, disciplined — but with no real relevance/omnichannel engine you've defended the value floor and ceded the digital future. Is that the question you framed?`,
                    coach_r2_overcap:       `You've blown through the €220M envelope — the cap is a covenant, not a suggestion. Re-allocate, or the CFO's 2.5x test breaks.`,
                    coach_r2_partner:       `A partnership lets someone else carry the capability and the upside; fine as a complement, weak as the whole bet. Who owns the advantage in three years?`,
                    coach_r2_pilot:         `A tiny pilot in everything, reassess in twelve months — that's the old maybe wearing a real-options costume. A pilot with no scale trigger is just delay.`,
                    coach_r2_token:         `A token slice in each declined bet so no one feels overruled is not strategy — it's conflict avoidance. Strategy is what you choose NOT to do; name it.`,

                    coach_r3_enabler:       `Leading with the lower-capital enabler banks an early proof point that funds and de-risks the bold build. That's how you sequence under uncertainty without freezing.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `Enabler first, both a scale trigger AND kill-criteria on each tranche — this is genuine staged commitment, not disguised hesitation. The board can see exactly what releases the next €40M and what stops it.`,
                    coach_r3_boldfirst:     `Going straight for the heaviest, slowest build sinks the most capital before any proof point — and a €100M+ write-down hits the covenant first. Bank a win before you bet the balance sheet.`,
                    coach_r3_parallel:      `Both bets at once burns cash and organisational capacity simultaneously and gives you no learning between them. Sequence is itself a strategic choice.`,
                    coach_r3_trigger:       `A scale trigger with no kill-criteria means you'll pour money in once you start and never stop. What result, decided NOW, kills the bet outright?`,
                    coach_r3_reassess:      `“Pilot and reassess at each board meeting” is staging with no gates — the maybe in a real-options costume. Commit to the trigger and the kill-criteria, or you haven't committed at all.`,
                    coach_r3_afterproof:    `Hiding the direction until the first proof point starves the 35% block of the certainty they need and lets opponents brief against you in the silence.`,
                    coach_r3_hedge:         `A broad “exploring options” review keeps everyone comfortable and tells the market you still haven't decided. Comfort is not a mandate.`,
                    coach_r3_covenant:      `Drawing the tranche with almost no headroom under the 2.5x test means one bad quarter triggers the covenant and forces a fire-sale of the bet. Keep room to be wrong.`,

                    coach_r4_certainty:     `Exactly right — you gave the prudent faction real certainty (gated milestones, kill-criteria, a downside cap) so they could vote yes without you diluting the bet. That converts dissent into an executable mandate.`,
